{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"In Review ICML 2026 workshop"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/885eb8eef3c4110598b307f9338ceb46718490a3.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"mehta|confident_and_wrong_silent_semantic_failure_in_deployed_coding_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["~Aman_Mehta2"]},"abstract":{"value":"Submit rate dramatically overstates agent capability: GPT-5 submits 100% of patches on\nSWE-bench but only 44% pass tests; Llama 4 submits 99% but resolves only 18%. We study\nthis gap across 1,750 agent trajectories—four frontier models on 50 SWE-bench Verified tasks\nspanning five repositories, each run five times, plus guard intervention runs—and identify a\nfailure mode we term silent semantic failure: the agent consistently submits confident-looking\npatches that fail all tests, with no behavioral variance to signal the problem. Llama 4 is the\nworst offender (29/50 tasks, SSFR: 71%); paradoxically, Gemini—despite the lowest submit\nrate—has the fewest silent semantic failures (SSFR: 16%) because it fails to submit rather than\nsubmitting wrong patches. We propose a five-category failure taxonomy grounded in test-verified\nresolve rates, show the behavioral consistency hierarchy (Claude CV: 17.7% < GPT-5: 30.3%\n< Gemini: 44.9% < Llama 4: 58.1%) holds across all five repositories, and demonstrate that\ntwo guard interventions do not significantly improve overall resolve rate. These findings imply\nthat submission-based evaluation masks the most dangerous failure mode, and that test-verified,\nmulti-run assessment is essential for safe deployment."},"title":{"value":"Confident and Wrong: Silent Semantic Failure in Deployed Coding Agents"},"authors":{"value":["Aman Mehta"]}},"tmdate":1778009618951,"pdate":1774508400000,"tcdate":1778009519598,"writers":["~Aman_Mehta2"],"signatures":["~Aman_Mehta2"],"forum":"fSwHVb5FOQ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":48357,"cdate":1778009519598,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1778009618951,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"fSwHVb5FOQ","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Human-AI Interaction, Human-Centered Evaluation, LLM Agents, Evaluation Methodologies"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM Evaluation; Coding Agents; Human-AI Interaction"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"Yes"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"N/A"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4 (Data Source and Analysis). The paper reports over 1,400 interaction logs, 81 students, and 13 multi-turn evaluation scenarios."},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"No"},"B4_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4 (Data Source and Analysis). Interaction logs were anonymized prior to analysis and annotation. The study used consented classroom data collected under IRB approval."},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"Sections 4 and 5. The paper describes the educational music-coding environment, interaction scenarios, RHCA dimensions, failure taxonomy, and annotation protocol used for evaluation."},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"section 6 and 7"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 5 (RHCA Behavioral Analysis). The paper reports descriptive statistics including failure distributions and inter-rater agreement statistics (Fleiss' κ)."},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"The paper introduces conceptual evaluation artifacts (e.g., failure tags and evaluation cards) but does not distribute datasets, software, or models requiring licensing discussions."},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4 (Data Source and Analysis). The interaction logs were collected in an IRB-approved educational research setting and used for research purposes consistent with the original data collection context."},"D3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4 (Data Source and Analysis). The paper states that consented interaction logs from an IRB-approved classroom study were used."},"D4_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4 (Data Source and Analysis). The study used interaction logs collected from an IRB-approved classroom study."},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"D1_elaboration":{"value":"Due to space limitations in the short paper format, full participant and annotation instructions were not included."},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"section 2 and 4"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"N/A"},"D2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4 (Data Source and Analysis). The paper describes the use of interaction data collected from an IRB-approved classroom study involving student participants. Compensation and recruitment procedures were handled under the approved study protocol."},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"AI assistants were used for language polishing only. All research design, analysis, annotation, and scientific claims were developed and verified by the authors."},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"N/A"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"Yes"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026evaluating,\ntitle={Evaluating Behavioral Failures in Coding Agents},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jj15f9ABMi},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Evaluating Behavioral Failures in Coding Agents"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","Data analysis","Position papers"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"Yes"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Coding agents are becoming increasingly autonomous, capable of generating, modifying, and executing code across multi-turn human--AI workflows. However, current evaluation pipelines still primarily rely on aggregate correctness metrics, preference-based ranking, repair success, or benchmark-driven evaluations, providing limited insight into behavioral failures that emerge across multi-turn coding interactions. In this work, we introduce RHCA, a human-centered framework for behavioral analysis of coding agents. RHCA organizes interaction analysis across four dimensions: Reasoning Transparency, Helpfulness, Consistency, and Context Alignment. We further introduce a structured failure taxonomy, RHCA-guided failure tags, and reusable evaluation cards for analyzing behavioral failures in multi-turn coding interactions. Overall, this work contributes a behaviorally granular and human-centered framework for analyzing coding-agent failures beyond aggregate evaluation."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"No"},"paper_type":{"value":"Short"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8a43f09c1af847430c1f138bb6b937f3244f8987.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Human-Centered NLP and Human-AI Interaction"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"yes"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"Yes"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696997222,"tcdate":1779764670097,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission11095/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission11095/Authors"],"forum":"jj15f9ABMi","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":11095,"cdate":1779764670097,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696997222,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"jj15f9ABMi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2026 Workshop VERICODEGEN Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fcf446419c146dccfb0d7aa5a501344c61026b27.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Coding Agents","LLMs","Trustworthy AI"]},"llm_review_consent":{"value":"Yes, I understand and accept that my submission may receive reviews generated by an LLM."},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN"},"paperhash":{"value":"mehta|silent_regressions_the_verification_gap_in_coding_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["~Aman_Mehta2"]},"abstract":{"value":"Coding agents increasingly make changes that no human reviews line by line, so the checks used to oversee them matter. The most common check is also the cheapest: did the run finish with a patch. We study what this signal misses. We ran four frontier models five times each on 100 SWE-bench Verified tasks, added runs under prompt interventions, and linked all 2,750 runs to per-test harness results, which lets us say why each patch failed rather than only that it did. We release this dataset. Completion carries little information about correctness: Claude and GPT-5 return a patch on 98% and 97% of runs but resolve 67% and 45%. Many of the failures completion hides are not idle runs but silent regressions, plausible patches that break tests which passed before the agent ran; 40% of Llama 4 runs produce one. Two test-free remedies did not help in our setting. Prompting GPT-5 to write and run a failing test before editing, on 50 tasks, made it run the suite far more often but left its resolve and regression rates statistically equivalent to baseline. A logistic monitor on trajectory features generalized across repositories but fell to chance within a single model. Targeted testing did help: running only the benchmark's bug-revealing tests, under 2% of the suite, reduced the share of accepted patches that break working code from 19% to 5%, against 10% for the same number of random tests. Cheap verification of coding agents therefore depends less on how many tests are run than on having the right ones, which in practice means a failing test that comes from outside the agent."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nmehta2026silent,\ntitle={Silent Regressions: The Verification Gap in Coding Agents},\nauthor={Aman Mehta},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on AI for Verifiable Coding},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=hEIt7k3Lny}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Silent Regressions: The Verification Gap in Coding Agents"},"authors":{"value":["Aman Mehta"]}},"tmdate":1792081902928,"pdate":1790773414831,"tcdate":1789100591624,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN/Submission58/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN/Submission58/Authors"],"forum":"hEIt7k3Lny","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":58,"cdate":1789100591624,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN/Submission58/-/Revision"],"mdate":1792081902928,"odate":1790773414831,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/VERICODEGEN","id":"hEIt7k3Lny","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.09863v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"advani|from_confident_closing_to_silent_failure_characterizing_false_success_in_llm_agents"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.09863"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2606-09863,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Laksh Advani},\n  title={From Confident Closing to Silent Failure: Characterizing False Success in LLM Agents},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2606.09863},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.09863}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"LLM agents can fail silently by asserting task completion when the environment state shows otherwise. We study this failure mode, false success, across two agent benchmarks: 9,876 tau2-bench trajectories from 8 model families and 1,879 AppWorld trajectories from 4 model families with text-independent ground truth. False success is common but varies by setting: 45--48% of failures in single-control tau2-bench domains, 3% in dual-control telecom, and 75.8% among AppWorld self-assessing coding-agent trajectories with explicit status claims. LLM judges fail reliably: no configuration across 5 judges, 5 prompt strategies, and full task specifications exceeds AUROC 0.65 on tau2-bench, and the same judges reach only 0.54 AUROC on AppWorld API-call traces. Judges rely on surface completion proxies -- confident closing language in tau2-bench and coarse action-sequence volume in AppWorld -- rather than verified state changes. Lightweight TF-IDF detectors achieve task-disjoint AUROC 0.83 on tau2-bench and 0.95 on AppWorld, recovering 4--8x more false successes than the best judge at the same flag rate with 3,300x lower latency. These results suggest that production monitoring should use lightweight, domain-calibrated detectors as triage signals rather than relying on LLM judges as the primary monitor for false success."},"title":{"value":"From Confident Closing to Silent Failure: Characterizing False Success in LLM Agents"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Laksh Advani","username":"~Laksh_Advani1"}]}},"tmdate":1790613548167,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2606-09863"],"tcdate":1790613545759,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Laksh_Advani1"],"forum":"NEXJEgE3nW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":158437,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1790613548167,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"NEXJEgE3nW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"FAGEN@ICML 2026 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/cc8b128052e9c9efe1298df297d7e533401e4745.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM agents false success agent failure modes LLM-as-judge agent evaluation trace diagnostics tool-use agents environment state verification silent failure confidence anchoring trajectory analysis failure detection programmatic reward customer-service agents tau2-bench agent monitoring lightweight classifiers TF-IDF detectors cross-domain generalization deployment triage"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN"},"paperhash":{"value":"advani|from_confident_closing_to_silent_failure_characterizing_false_success_in_llm_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["~Laksh_Advani1"]},"abstract":{"value":"LLM agents can fail silently by asserting task completion even when the underlying environment state shows the action did not occur. We study this failure mode, \\emph{false success}, on 9,876 tau2-bench trajectories across 8 model families and 3 customer-service domains. We introduce a three-class decomposition of failed trajectories---false success, honest failure, and ambiguous---validated against human annotation (91.5\\% agreement, $\\kappa=0.86$). False success is common but environment-dependent: it accounts for 45--48\\% of failures in single-control airline and retail domains, but only 3\\% in dual-control telecom. LLM-as-judge evaluation systematically misses this failure mode: across 5 judges, 5 prompt strategies, and a strong baseline with full task specifications, no judge exceeds AUROC 0.65. Judges anchor on confident closing language, scoring assertion-rich traces 0.27--0.36 more ``completed'' regardless of tool evidence. Lightweight TF-IDF detectors instead achieve task-disjoint AUROC 0.83 with sub-millisecond inference. Although zero-shot cross-domain transfer is limited (AUROC 0.69), few-shot target-domain calibration recovers performance, reaching AUROC 0.79 with 50 examples per class and 0.83 with 100 examples per class. These results suggest that production monitoring should treat lightweight detectors as efficient, domain-calibrated triage filters rather than relying on LLM judges as primary detectors for false success."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nadvani2026from,\ntitle={From Confident Closing to Silent Failure: Characterizing False Success in {LLM} Agents},\nauthor={Laksh Advani},\nbooktitle={Workshop on Failure Modes of Agentic AI at ICML 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=cPxxSqUFYj}\n}"},"title":{"value":"From Confident Closing to Silent Failure: Characterizing False Success in LLM Agents"},"authors":{"value":["Laksh Advani"]}},"tmdate":1784105860321,"pdate":1780441948246,"tcdate":1777959451059,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/Submission42/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/Submission42/Authors"],"forum":"cPxxSqUFYj","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":42,"cdate":1777959451059,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/Submission42/-/Revision"],"mdate":1784105860321,"odate":1780441948246,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN","id":"cPxxSqUFYj","version":2}],"count":10000}